The following formulas may be useful:

$\int x^n dx = \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C$, where $n \neq -1$ is a fixed number, $C$ refers to an arbitrary constant;

$\int \frac{dx}{x} = \ln|x| + C$, where $C$ stands for an arbitrary constant;

$\int \frac{dx}{(a^2+x^2)^{3/2}} = \frac{x}{a^2\sqrt{a^2+x^2}} + C$, where *a* – constant, $C$ – arbitrary constant;

$(1+x)^{\gamma} \approx 1 + \gamma x + \frac{\gamma(\gamma-1)}{2}x^2$, for $|x| \ll 1$ and any value of $\gamma$;

$\tan x \approx \sin x \approx x$, for $|x| \ll 1$;

$\ln(1+x) \approx x$, for $|x| \ll 1$.

***List of physical constants***

Speed of light in vacuum $c = 3.00 \cdot 10^{8}$ m/s;

Reduced Planck constant $\hbar = 1.05 \cdot 10^{-34}$ J·s;

Magnetic constant $\mu_0 = 4\pi \cdot 10^{-7}$ H·m;

Electric constant $\varepsilon_0 = 8.85 \cdot 10^{-12}$ F/m;

Stefan-Boltzmann constant $\sigma = 5.67 \cdot 10^{-8}\ W/(m^2 \cdot K^4)$;

Elementary charge $e = 1.60 \cdot 10^{-19}$ C;

Electron mass $m_e = 9.11 \cdot 10^{-31}$ kg.
